"Shows the fraction of observed values at or below each numeric threshold without binning or smoothing.\n\nUse when: Readers need percentile comparisons or the share below a threshold. Two samples should be compared without choosing histogram bins.\n\nAvoid when: The target quantity requires censoring adjustment or a smoothed density shape.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes observed values. Step height encodes cumulative sample proportion, with jumps proportional to tied observations.\n\nBuild: Sort nonmissing observations and count ties. Calculate cumulative counts divided by the included sample size. Draw a nondecreasing step curve and label useful threshold intersections.\n\nCheck: Check the curve ends at one or 100%. Verify tied observations produce correctly sized jumps. State the inequality convention and treatment of missing or weighted observations.\n\nPitfalls: Interpolation can imply probabilities between values that were never observed. Crossings mean neither sample dominates at every threshold; use direct threshold annotations or separate panels for clarity.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic delivery times by showing what fraction arrives within 24 and 48 hours.\n\nAlternatives: Histogram, Quantile-quantile plot, Kaplan–Meier curve."
